= Metabolism — Technical =

== Pathways and energy coupling ==
Metabolic networks comprise enzyme-catalyzed reactions connected by metabolites and cofactors. Anabolism and catabolism describe broad functional tendencies; coupling through ATP hydrolysis, redox cofactors or ion gradients makes some energetically unfavorable individual steps feasible within the full network.

== Flux versus concentration ==
A constant metabolite pool may coexist with nonzero production and consumption fluxes. Reaction rates depend on substrates, products, enzymes, regulation and compartmentalization. A measured metabolite concentration alone therefore does not determine pathway flux.

== Model boundaries and evidence ==
Constraint-based analysis uses stoichiometric balances and specified exchanges; kinetic models additionally make assumptions about rate laws. Neither class establishes a biological mechanism merely because it fits one dataset. Isotope labeling, perturbation studies and independent measurements can constrain or falsify specific predictions.

